  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-014",
   "finding": "report #20; report §3 flag 11",
   "old": "times the ISSCC 2021 chip, so Sony's research is roughly level with the 45% claimed for RoboSense's 2026 chip.\n- **Why automotive pitch stays near 10 µm while research reaches 2.5 µm** (IEDM 2022). There are two reasons.\n  - NIR absorption in silicon needs several micrometres of depth (§C4)",
   "new": "times the ISSCC 2021 chip. Sony's research is therefore roughly level with the 45% claimed for RoboSense's 2026 chip, whose wavelength is not stated: slightly behind it if that figure is at 940 nm, and ahead if it is at 905 nm, where the table below lists an unconfirmed 51.0% for Sony's pixel.\n- **Why automotive pitch stays near 10 µm while research reaches 2.5 µm** (IEDM 2022). There are two reasons.\n  - NIR absorption in silicon needs tens of micrometres of path (absorption length $1/\\alpha\\approx35$ µm at 905 nm and ≈55 µm at 940 nm, §C4)",
   "why": "RoboSense's 45% has no wavelength; the comparison depends on it, and Sony's 905 nm figure is unconfirmed. §C4 gives absorption lengths of 35 and 55 µm, not several micrometres."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-015",
   "finding": "symbol scheme",
   "old": "$$N_{\\rm die}=\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4S}-\\frac{\\pi d}{\\sqrt{2S}} \\tag{77}$$\n\n  where $d$ is the wafer diameter (mm) and $S$ the die area (mm²).",
   "new": "$$N_{\\rm die}=\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4A_{\\rm die}}-\\frac{\\pi d}{\\sqrt{2A_{\\rm die}}} \\tag{77}$$\n\n  where $d$ is the wafer diameter (mm) and $A_{\\rm die}$ the die area (mm²).",
   "why": "S is reserved for the sweep rate report-wide; the die area becomes A_die."
  },
