   "why": "Round-trip delay is tau = 2R/c."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-032",
   "finding": "report #9",
   "old": "- Effective lines went from 128 to 520 to 2,160 per chip; a 4,320-line system uses two chips. \"Lines\" may involve binning (pooling SPADs into one output) or interleaving (combining samples taken at offset positions), so they are not automatically independent physical detector channels.\n- Point rates: 25–41 Mpts/s (millions of points per second).",
   "new": "- Effective lines per chip went from 192 (Sony IMX459, 2021) to 520 (IMX479, 2025) to 2,160 (Hesai Picasso, 2026); the 4,320-line Hesai ETX uses two chips. \"Lines\" may involve binning (pooling SPADs into one output) or interleaving (combining samples taken at offset positions), so they are not automatically independent physical detector channels.\n- Point rates reach 25 Mpts/s (millions of points per second) for IMX479 (§C5a) and 41 Mpts/s for the 2,160-beam RoboSense EM4 (§C7a).",
   "why": "Names the devices and years; 128 matched no SPAD-SoC, and 41 Mpts/s is the 2,160-beam EM4."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-033",
   "finding": "report #30",
   "old": "Seven factors, (a) to (g), limit how far the approach can go. Sunlight, item (d), is the dominant daytime limit; the étendue discussion further below ranks the binding constraints for a wide-field staring receiver.",
   "new": "Six factors limit how far the approach can go: (a) die size; (b) the photon budget, worked through in the link budget (c); (d) sunlight, with the mitigations of (d′); (e) histogram memory; (f) avalanche power; and (g) cost. Sunlight is the dominant daytime limit. For a wide-field staring receiver, the étendue discussion further below ranks four constraints as binding: aperture, silicon area, histogram memory and avalanche power.",
   "why": "(c) is the worked budget of (b) and (d') the mitigations of (d): six limits, matching the four binding ones of the opener."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-034",
   "finding": "report #33",
   "old": "- Quadrupling the point count halves the 10%-target range, because Eq. (79) gives",
   "new": "- Eq. (79) is §C0 Eq. (42) with $E_{\\rm pt}=P_{\\rm opt}/\\dot N_{\\rm pts}$; at fixed $P_{\\rm opt}$ it keeps $\\dot N_{\\rm pts}R_{\\max}^2$ constant, the range-weighted point rate of §C3b Eq. (62). Quadrupling the point count halves the 10%-target range, because Eq. (79) gives",
   "why": "Introduces Eq. (79) as §C0 Eq. (42) and §C3b Eq. (62) in this section's notation."
  },
