  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-045",
   "finding": "report #11",
   "old": "for example $1.35\\times10^{8}/9=1.5\\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹. The first row's background is the 940 nm, 10 nm, ρ = 0.9 value scaled by twice the filter width and one ninth of the reflectance: $6.1\\times10^{8}\\times2\\times(0.1/0.9)=1.35\\times10^{8}$ s⁻¹.\n- **Singles** (events with no coincidence requirement) are the macro-pixel rate, $N_{\\rm mp}r=r_{\\rm bg}$.\n- **$n_{\\rm th}$-fold accidentals** use Eq. (84), $\\binom{N_{\\rm mp}}{n_{\\rm th}}\\,n_{\\rm th}\\,r^{n_{\\rm th}}\\tau_c^{\\,n_{\\rm th}-1}$, with $\\tau_c=10^{-9}$ s and $\\binom{9}{2}=36$, $\\binom{9}{3}=84$, $\\binom{9}{4}=126$. First row: 2-fold $36\\times2\\times(1.5\\times10^{7})^2\\times10^{-9}=1.6\\times10^{7}$; 3-fold $84\\times3\\times(1.5\\times10^{7})^3\\times10^{-18}=8.5\\times10^{5}$, which is $1.35\\times10^{8}/(8.5\\times10^{5})\\approx160$ times below the singles; 4-fold $126\\times4\\times(1.5\\times10^{7})^4\\times10^{-27}=2.6\\times10^{4}$. The second row, with $r=6.8\\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹, gives $3.3\\times10^{8}$, $7.9\\times10^{7}$ (a factor $6.1\\times10^{8}/(7.9\\times10^{7})\\approx8$ below the singles) and $1.1\\times10^{7}$ (about 57–58×).",
   "new": "for example $1.32\\times10^{8}/9=1.46\\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹. The first row's background is the 940 nm, 10 nm, ρ = 0.9 value of (d), $5.93\\times10^{8}$ s⁻¹, scaled by twice the filter width and one ninth of the reflectance: $5.93\\times10^{8}\\times2\\times(0.1/0.9)=1.32\\times10^{8}$ s⁻¹.\n- **Singles** (events with no coincidence requirement) are the macro-pixel rate, $N_{\\rm mp}r=r_{\\rm bg}$.\n- **$n_{\\rm th}$-fold accidentals** use Eq. (84), $\\binom{N_{\\rm mp}}{n_{\\rm th}}\\,n_{\\rm th}\\,r^{n_{\\rm th}}\\tau_c^{\\,n_{\\rm th}-1}$, with $\\tau_c=10^{-9}$ s and $\\binom{9}{2}=36$, $\\binom{9}{3}=84$, $\\binom{9}{4}=126$. First row: 2-fold $36\\times2\\times(1.46\\times10^{7})^2\\times10^{-9}=1.5\\times10^{7}$; 3-fold $84\\times3\\times(1.46\\times10^{7})^3\\times10^{-18}=7.8\\times10^{5}$, which is $1.32\\times10^{8}/(7.8\\times10^{5})\\approx170$ times below the singles; 4-fold $126\\times4\\times(1.46\\times10^{7})^4\\times10^{-27}=2.3\\times10^{4}$. The second row, with $r=5.93\\times10^{8}/9=6.59\\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹, gives $3.1\\times10^{8}$, $7.2\\times10^{7}$ (a factor $5.93\\times10^{8}/(7.2\\times10^{7})\\approx8$ below the singles) and $9.5\\times10^{6}$ (a factor $5.93\\times10^{8}/(9.5\\times10^{6})\\approx62$).",
   "why": "Recomputes the derivations from 5.93e8 s^-1, with the substitutions shown."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-046",
   "finding": "report #27",
   "old": "Let $\\bar n$ be the mean number of detected signal photons per shot. With Poisson photon statistics, the probability of detecting at least three is one minus the probability of detecting zero, one or two: $P(\\ge3)=1-e^{-\\bar n}(1+\\bar n+\\bar n^2/2)$. A mean of $\\bar n=5$ detected photons per shot gives $1-e^{-5}\\times18.5=0.88$; a mean of 2 gives only $1-e^{-2}\\times5=0.32$.",
   "new": "Let $\\bar n$ be the mean number of detected signal photons per shot (the per-pulse count $N_{s,1}$ of §C0 Eq. (23)). With Poisson photon statistics, the probability of detecting at least $n_{\\rm th}=3$ is one minus the probability of detecting zero, one or two: $P(\\ge3)=1-e^{-\\bar n}(1+\\bar n+\\bar n^2/2)$. A mean of $\\bar n=5$ detected photons per shot gives $1-e^{-5}\\times18.5=0.88$; a mean of 2 gives only $1-e^{-2}\\times5=0.32$. At 200 m on a 10% target, (c) gives ≈21–33 photons per point, i.e. $\\bar n\\approx2.1$–3.3 per shot over the ≈10 shots of a point, so a 3-fold requirement would catch only $1-e^{-2.1}\\times5.305=0.35$ to $1-e^{-3.3}\\times9.745=0.64$ of the shots.",
   "why": "Makes n_th = 3 explicit and ties Lesson 2 to the (c) link budget."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-047",
   "finding": "report #11 and §3 flag 5",
   "old": "In the bright 940 nm/10 nm case ($r=6.1\\times10^{8}/9=6.8\\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹ per SPAD) a passively recharged SPAD with Sony's 6 ns is dead $1-e^{-0.41}=34\\%$ of the time. With active recharge it is dead 29% at 6 ns ($0.41/1.41$), 17% at 3 ns ($0.20/1.20$) and 58% at 20 ns ($r\\tau_d=1.36$, and $1.36/2.36=0.58$).",
   "new": "In the bright 940 nm/10 nm case ($r=5.93\\times10^{8}/9=6.59\\times10^{7}$ s⁻¹ per SPAD, so $r\\tau_d=0.40$ at 6 ns) a passively recharged SPAD with Sony's 6 ns is dead $1-e^{-0.40}=33\\%$ of the time, as in (d). With active recharge it is dead 29% at 6 ns ($0.40/1.40$), 17% at 3 ns ($0.20/1.20$) and 57% at 20 ns ($r\\tau_d=1.32$, and $1.32/2.32=0.57$).",
   "why": "Uses 5.93e8 s^-1: 33% passive (as in (d)), 29/17/57% active."
  },
  {
   "id": "06_C5_C5a_C6-048",
   "finding": "report #25",
   "old": "- **Multi-shot time-correlated single-photon counting (TCSPC) histograms**, i.e. accumulating photon arrival times over many shots, with matched filtering (correlating the histogram with the expected pulse shape) and constant-false-alarm-rate (CFAR) peak detection, which sets the detection threshold from the measured noise floor so that false alarms stay at a fixed rate. Background forms a flat floor that averages down as $\\sqrt{N_{\\rm shots}}$, where $N_{\\rm shots}$ is the number of laser shots accumulated into one histogram.",
   "new": "- **Multi-shot time-correlated single-photon counting (TCSPC) histograms** accumulate photon arrival times over many shots. Matched filtering correlates the histogram with the expected pulse shape. Constant-false-alarm-rate (CFAR) peak detection sets the detection threshold from the measured noise floor, so that false alarms stay at a fixed rate. The background forms a flat floor that grows in proportion to $N_{\\rm shots}$, the number of laser shots accumulated into one histogram, while its relative fluctuation falls as $1/\\sqrt{N_{\\rm shots}}$; the signal-to-noise ratio of the return therefore grows as $\\sqrt{N_{\\rm shots}}$.",
   "why": "The floor grows with N_shots; its relative fluctuation falls as 1/sqrt(N_shots). Splits the 50-word sentence."
  },
