   "finding": "report #19",
   "old": "- **1550 nm ToF:** about 10× the eye-safe average power and about 3× less sunlight, at 2–3× the cost. The factor 3 is in solar irradiance ($0.27/0.82\\approx0.33$). Counted in photons per nm, which is what sets the background count, 1,550 nm has 0.67× the background of 905 nm (§C4c), about 1.5× less, because each 1,550 nm photon carries less energy.",
   "new": "- **1550 nm ToF:** about 10× the eye-safe average power and 2.6–3× less solar irradiance, at 2–3× the cost. On the ±10 nm band averages of §C4c, 1,550 nm has $0.270/0.693=0.39$ of the 905 nm solar irradiance (≈2.6× less). Counted in photons per nm, which is what sets the background count, that is $0.39\\times1{,}550/905=0.67$ of the 905 nm value (≈1.5× less), because each 1,550 nm photon carries less energy. At the exact wavelengths the two ratios are $0.270/0.817=0.33$ (3× less) and $0.33\\times1{,}550/905=0.57$.",
   "why": "Puts irradiance and photon ratios on one basis (band averages), with the exact-wavelength values alongside."
