   "finding": "report #18 (partial)",
   "old": "- In sunlight a scanned column dissipates about 0.2–0.55 W; a staring VGA (640 × 480) SPAD array about **5–12 W**. The avalanche power is $P_{\\rm av}=N_{\\rm pix}\\,r_{\\rm av}\\,E_{\\rm av}$, where $N_{\\rm pix}$ is the number of active pixels, $r_{\\rm av}$ the avalanche rate per pixel (s⁻¹) and $E_{\\rm av}$ the energy per avalanche (J).\n  - The column figure corresponds to about $2.5\\times10^{8}$ avalanches/s in each of 520 macro-pixels: $520\\times2.5\\times10^{8}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\times1.6\\ {\\rm pJ}=0.21$ W, and 0.51 W at 3.9 pJ.",
   "new": "- In sunlight a scanned column dissipates about 0.2–0.5 W; a staring VGA (640 × 480) SPAD array about **5–12 W**. The avalanche power is $P_{\\rm av}=N_{\\rm pix}\\,r_{\\rm av}\\,E_{\\rm av}$, where $N_{\\rm pix}$ is the number of active pixels, $r_{\\rm av}$ the avalanche rate per pixel (s⁻¹) and $E_{\\rm av}$ the energy per avalanche (J).\n  - Roughly, only photons that find a SPAD armed trigger a full avalanche, so the avalanche rate is close to the recorded count rate (Eq. (76) for paralyzable SPADs) rather than the incident photon rate. The column figure assumes about $2.5\\times10^{8}$ avalanches/s in each of 520 macro-pixels, near the $3.66\\times10^{8}\\times0.784=2.9\\times10^{8}$ s⁻¹ recorded in the model's central case of (d) [E]: $520\\times2.5\\times10^{8}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}\\times1.6\\ {\\rm pJ}=0.21$ W, and 0.51 W at 3.9 pJ.",
   "why": "Headline matches the derived 0.21-0.51 W; ties 2.5e8 s^-1 to the recorded (not incident) rate of the central case."
